{"step_index":53,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:54:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:24:35+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:24:35+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/agent-browser/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 1721\nTotal Bytes: 75173\nShowing lines 1 to 150\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # agent-browser\r\n2: \r\n3: Browser automation CLI for AI agents. Fast native Rust CLI.\r\n4: \r\n5: [![skills.sh](https://skills.sh/b/vercel-labs/agent-browser)](https://skills.sh/vercel-labs/agent-browser)\r\n6: \r\n7: ## Installation\r\n8: \r\n9: ### Global Installation (recommended)\r\n10: \r\n11: Installs the native Rust binary:\r\n12: \r\n13: ```bash\r\n14: npm install -g agent-browser\r\n15: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n16: ```\r\n17: \r\n18: ### Project Installation (local dependency)\r\n19: \r\n20: For projects that want to pin the version in `package.json`:\r\n21: \r\n22: ```bash\r\n23: npm install agent-browser\r\n24: agent-browser install\r\n25: ```\r\n26: \r\n27: Then use via `package.json` scripts or by invoking `agent-browser` directly.\r\n28: \r\n29: ### Homebrew (macOS)\r\n30: \r\n31: ```bash\r\n32: brew install agent-browser\r\n33: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n34: ```\r\n35: \r\n36: ### Cargo (Rust)\r\n37: \r\n38: ```bash\r\n39: cargo install agent-browser\r\n40: agent-browser install  # Download Chrome from Chrome for Testing (first time only)\r\n41: ```\r\n42: \r\n43: ### From Source\r\n44: \r\n45: Requires Node.js 24+, pnpm 11+, and Rust.\r\n46: \r\n47: ```bash\r\n48: git clone https://github.com/vercel-labs/agent-browser\r\n49: cd agent-browser\r\n50: pnpm install\r\n51: pnpm build\r\n52: pnpm build:native   # Requires Rust (https://rustup.rs)\r\n53: pnpm link --global  # Makes agent-browser available globally\r\n54: agent-browser install\r\n55: ```\r\n56: \r\n57: ### Linux Dependencies\r\n58: \r\n59: On Linux, install system dependencies:\r\n60: \r\n61: ```bash\r\n62: agent-browser install --with-deps\r\n63: ```\r\n64: \r\n65: This exits nonzero if the package manager cannot install every required browser library.\r\n66: \r\n67: ### Updating\r\n68: \r\n69: Upgrade to the latest version:\r\n70: \r\n71: ```bash\r\n72: agent-browser upgrade\r\n73: ```\r\n74: \r\n75: Detects your installation method (npm, Homebrew, or Cargo) and runs the appropriate update command automatically.\r\n76: \r\n77: ### Requirements\r\n78: \r\n79: - **Chrome** - Run `agent-browser install` to download Chrome from [Chrome for Testing](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/chrome-for-testing/) (Google's official automation channel). Existing Chrome, Brave, Playwright, and Puppeteer installations are detected automatically. No Playwright or Node.js required for the daemon.\r\n80: - **Node.js 24+ and pnpm 11+** - Only needed when building from source.\r\n81: - **Rust** - Only needed when building from source (see From Source above).\r\n82: \r\n83: ## Quick Start\r\n84: \r\n85: ```bash\r\n86: agent-browser open example.com\r\n87: agent-browser snapshot                    # Get accessibility tree with refs\r\n88: agent-browser click @e2                   # Click by ref from snapshot\r\n89: agent-browser fill @e3 \"test@example.com\" # Fill by ref\r\n90: agent-browser get text @e1                # Get text by ref\r\n91: agent-browser screenshot page.png\r\n92: agent-browser close\r\n93: ```\r\n94: \r\n95: Clicks fail early when another element covers the target's click point, for example a consent banner or modal. Dismiss or interact with the reported covering element, then take a fresh snapshot before retrying the original ref.\r\n96: \r\n97: Headless Chromium screenshots hide native scrollbars for consistent image output. Pass `--hide-scrollbars false` when launching to keep native scrollbars visible.\r\n98: \r\n99: ### Traditional Selectors (also supported)\r\n100: \r\n101: ```bash\r\n102: agent-browser click \"#submit\"\r\n103: agent-browser fill \"#email\" \"test@example.com\"\r\n104: agent-browser find role button click --name \"Submit\"\r\n105: ```\r\n106: \r\n107: ## Commands\r\n108: \r\n109: ### Core Commands\r\n110: \r\n111: ```bash\r\n112: agent-browser open                    # Launch browser (no navigation); stays on about:blank\r\n113: agent-browser open <url>              # Launch + navigate to URL (aliases: goto, navigate)\r\n114: agent-browser read [url]              # Fetch agent-readable text, or read rendered active-tab DOM\r\n115: agent-browser click <sel>             # Click element (--new-tab to open in new tab)\r\n116: agent-browser dblclick <sel>          # Double-click element\r\n117: agent-browser focus <sel>             # Focus element\r\n118: agent-browser type <sel> <text>       # Type into element\r\n119: agent-browser fill <sel> <text>       # Clear and fill\r\n120: agent-browser press <key>             # Press key (Enter, Tab, Control+a) (alias: key)\r\n121: agent-browser keyboard type <text>    # Type with real keystrokes (no selector, current focus)\r\n122: agent-browser keyboard inserttext <text>  # Insert text without key events (no selector)\r\n123: agent-browser keydown <key>           # Hold key down\r\n124: agent-browser keyup <key>             # Release key\r\n125: agent-browser hover <sel>             # Hover element\r\n126: agent-browser select <sel> <val>      # Select dropdown option\r\n127: agent-browser check <sel>             # Check checkbox\r\n128: agent-browser uncheck <sel>           # Uncheck checkbox\r\n129: agent-browser scroll <dir> [px]       # Scroll (up/down/left/right, --selector <sel>)\r\n130: agent-browser scrollintoview <sel>    # Scroll element into view (alias: scrollinto)\r\n131: agent-browser drag <src> <tgt>        # Drag and drop\r\n132: agent-browser upload <sel> <files>    # Upload files\r\n133: agent-browser screenshot [path]       # Take screenshot (--full for full page, saves to a temporary directory if no path)\r\n134: agent-browser screenshot --annotate   # Annotated screenshot with numbered element labels\r\n135: agent-browser screenshot --screenshot-dir ./shots    # Save to custom directory\r\n136: agent-browser screenshot --screenshot-format jpeg --screenshot-quality 80\r\n137: agent-browser pdf <path>              # Save as PDF\r\n138: agent-browser snapshot                # Accessibility tree with refs (best for AI)\r\n139: agent-browser eval <js>               # Run JavaScript (-b for base64, --stdin for piped input)\r\n140: agent-browser connect <port>          # Connect to browser via CDP\r\n141: agent-browser stream enable [--port <port>]  # Start runtime WebSocket streaming\r\n142: agent-browser stream status           # Show runtime streaming state and bound port\r\n143: agent-browser stream disable          # Stop runtime WebSocket streaming\r\n144: agent-browser close                   # Close browser (aliases: quit, exit)\r\n145: agent-browser close --all             # Close all active sessions\r\n146: agent-browser chat \"<instruction>\"    # AI chat: natural language browser control (single-shot)\r\n147: agent-browser chat                    # AI chat: interactive REPL mode\r\n148: ```\r\n149: \r\n150: ### Get Info\r\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":54,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:54:35Z","thinking":"**Defining Agent-Browser's Role**\n\nMy initial focus is on defining the core function of agent-browser. It's a fast Rust CLI and daemon. My current thinking revolves around how it empowers AI agents to automate and interact with web browsers efficiently. We're talking accessibility tree snapshots, references, and CDP. Right now, I am figuring out how to let agents open web pages, click elements...\n\n\n**Refining Capabilities List**\n\nI've expanded on the feature set to include filling elements, capturing annotated screenshots, evaluating JavaScript, and streaming browser sessions to reduce token overhead. I am also investigating hardware requirements. It looks like it doesn't need a dedicated GPU, it can run on CPU using headless Chrome. RAM should be around 1-2GB for Chrome and the daemon. Now, I am validating these parameters with a list of 13 other agents. I'm focusing on their summaries, GPU needs, and RAM. Finally, I will structure a clear summary and send it back.\n\n\n**Analyzing Agent Frameworks**\n\nI'm now investigating other agent frameworks to inform the design. Unsloth, a platform for LLM fine-tuning and inference, is promising for its performance gains. ComfyUI, a visual AI creation engine, also looks intriguing for its modular approach. It's becoming clear that this requires CPU and moderate RAM. The optimal environment will be 4GB-8GB system RAM. I'm focusing on hardware requirements, specifically GPU dependency.\n\n\n**Evaluating Hardware Requirements**\n\nI'm now analyzing the hardware needs for diffusion architectures. The standard generation is GPU-dependent, with CPU as a fallback. For diffusion models, I'm noting 6GB-16GB+ VRAM recommendations. The system RAM should range from 8GB to 16GB. Regarding the voice agent, Speech-to-Speech, it's CPU-compatible. However, a CUDA GPU or Apple Silicon is better for low-latency realtime interaction. For CPU use, RAM falls in the 4GB-8GB range.\n\n\n**Assessing Hardware for LLMs**\n\nMy focus is shifting to incorporating local LLMs and TTS models in pipelines. I see a need for around 6GB-12GB+ of VRAM/RAM for this. OmniVoice, the multilingual TTS model, looks promising, even if it prefers a GPU, and ViMax, a video generation framework, emphasizes API orchestration. I will analyze their hardware considerations further.\n\n\n**Determining Resource Needs**\n\nI'm now examining the memory and processing demands of graphify and Pixelle-Video. Graphify's CPU-based operation, specifically its reliance on tree-sitter AST parsing, allows for a relatively modest 2GB-4GB of RAM. Pixelle-Video, on the other hand, highlights the flexibility of a cloud-based solution, where a dedicated GPU is not necessary. Local execution of Pixelle-Video's image workflows via ComfyUI necessitates an NVIDIA GPU with 8GB-16GB+ of VRAM, making it the more resource-intensive option.\n\n\n**Estimating RAM requirements**\n\nI'm now determining RAM and VRAM necessities for video and TTS workflows. API or cloud-based applications will require at least 4-8GB system RAM, while local ComfyUI necessitates 16GB+ and 8-16GB+ respectively. Scrapling, as a CPU-based system, needs 1-2GB for HTTP requests, and 2-4GB+ for browsers. Findskill, as a lightweight tool, requires less than 512MB of system RAM.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"Here is the analysis of the README.md files for each of the 13 requested repositories located in `C:\\Users\\renu5\\Downloads\\find-skill-repo\\all github repos`:\n\n---\n\n### 1. **ruflo**\n- **Summary:** Ruflo is an agent meta-harness and execution layer for Claude Code and Codex that coordinates 100+ specialized agents, swarms, and persistent vector memory (AgentDB/HNSW). It automates multi-agent collaboration, background workers, and zero-trust cross-machine agent federation without manual orchestration.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (Runs on CPU as a Node.js/Rust orchestration harness; optional GPU acceleration is available if routing to local models via Ollama/vLLM or RuVector GPU search).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~512 MB – 2 GB RAM** (standard developer environment).\n\n---\n\n### 2. **unsloth**\n- **Summary:** Unsloth is a high-performance LLM training, fine-tuning, and inference platform available as both a web interface (Unsloth Studio) and a Python library (Unsloth Core). It accelerates model training up to 2x–12x while cutting VRAM usage by 70%–80% across 500+ text, vision, audio, and embedding models.\n- **Requires GPU:** **Yes** for model training and full GPU acceleration (NVIDIA RTX/Blackwell, AMD ROCm, Intel Arc, or Apple Silicon via MLX), though CPU is supported for basic chat/data recipes and GGUF inference mode.\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~6 GB – 8 GB VRAM** for fine-tuning smaller models (16 GB – 24 GB+ VRAM for larger models); **~8 GB – 16 GB system RAM** for CPU execution.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **comfyui**\n- **Summary:** ComfyUI is a modular, node/graph-based visual AI creation engine for designing and executing generative workflows across image, video, audio, and 3D models. It offers granular pipeline control for architectures like Stable Diffusion, Flux, Wan, and Hunyuan with smart memory offloading and API support.\n- **Requires GPU:** **Recommended/Practically Required** for standard generative workloads, though it can run on CPU using the `--cpu` flag (slow).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~1 GB VRAM** minimum with smart offloading (6 GB – 16 GB+ VRAM recommended for modern diffusion models); **~8 GB – 16 GB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **speech-to-speech**\n- **Summary:** Speech-to-Speech is a low-latency, modular voice-agent pipeline (VAD → STT → LLM → TTS) exposed via an OpenAI Realtime-compatible WebSocket/WebRTC API. It provides swappable local and cloud backends (such as Silero VAD, Parakeet/Whisper, and Qwen3-TTS/Kokoro/Pocket TTS) for building real-time voice bots and robotic conversation systems.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (supports CPU fallbacks for all stages), but a CUDA GPU or Apple Silicon (MPS/MLX) is strongly recommended for low-latency real-time performance.\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~4 GB – 8 GB system RAM** for CPU-based pipelines / cloud LLM APIs; **~6 GB – 12 GB+ VRAM/RAM** if hosting local LLMs and TTS models on-device.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **OmniVoice**\n- **Summary:** OmniVoice is a massively multilingual zero-shot text-to-speech (TTS) diffusion language model supporting over 600 languages with fast inference speeds (RTF ~0.025). It supports high-quality voice cloning from short audio references, attribute-based voice design (gender, age, pitch, accent), and fine-grained non-verbal expression control.\n- **Requires GPU:** **Yes / Strongly Recommended** for practical inference speed (supports NVIDIA CUDA, Apple Silicon MPS, and Intel Arc XPU), though standard PyTorch CPU execution is supported.\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~4 GB VRAM** minimum for GPU inference (tested on 4 GB Intel Arc A310 / consumer NVIDIA GPUs); **~8 GB – 16 GB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **ViMax**\n- **Summary:** ViMax is an agentic multi-agent video generation framework that autonomously transforms ideas, novels, or screenplays into complete multi-shot videos. It orchestrates scriptwriting, storyboarding, multi-camera consistency checking, and video synthesis through an interactive TUI or pipeline scripts connecting to frontier generation APIs.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (operates as a Python orchestration framework calling remote cloud APIs such as OpenAI, Gemini, Google Veo, Nano Banana, or Kling).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~4 GB – 8 GB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **graphify**\n- **Summary:** Graphify converts codebases, documentation, PDFs, and media into queryable knowledge graphs and interactive HTML visualizations. It utilizes tree-sitter AST parsing and community detection algorithms to trace connections, shortest paths, and architectural relationships across ~40 programming languages without vector embeddings.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (runs locally on CPU via tree-sitter AST; optional semantic passes on documents/media utilize assistant models or external APIs).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~2 GB – 4 GB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Pixelle-Video**\n- **Summary:** Pixelle-Video is an automated AI short-video generation engine and Streamlit WebUI that creates complete narrated videos with visuals, subtitles, and background music from a single topic keyword or script. It supports flexible atomic execution pipelines using cloud generation services (RunningHub, DashScope, OpenAI, Kling) or local ComfyUI workflows.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** dedicated GPU required when using cloud rendering or direct APIs; an **NVIDIA GPU (8 GB – 16 GB+ VRAM)** is required if running local ComfyUI image/video/TTS generation workflows.\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~4 GB – 8 GB system RAM** for API/cloud mode; **16 GB+ system RAM and 8 GB – 16 GB+ VRAM** for local ComfyUI generation.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Scrapling**\n- **Summary:** Scrapling is an adaptive web scraping and crawling framework engineered to handle everything from single HTTP/stealth requests to large-scale concurrent crawls with pause/resume support. It includes built-in anti-bot bypass for Cloudflare Turnstile, automatic element relocation when page structures change, proxy rotation, and an MCP server for AI agents.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (pure CPU-based Python library and headless browser automation).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~1 GB – 2 GB system RAM** for HTTP requests; **~2 GB – 4 GB+ RAM** when running concurrent headless Chromium/Playwright browsers.\n\n---\n\n### 10. **findskill**\n- **Summary:** findskill is a Windows-compatible fork of the find-skills tool for Claude Code that fixes empty-output issues when searching for agent skills on Windows. It updates the skill instructions to execute search and discovery commands via PowerShell rather than standard Git Bash.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (Markdown configuration and lightweight Node.js/PowerShell commands).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **<512 MB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 11. **gstack**\n- **Summary:** gstack is an open-source suite of skills and structured workflow roles for Claude Code and other AI coding agents that simulate a full virtual engineering team. It provides specialized slash commands for product brainstorming, CEO/eng/design reviews, browser-based QA automation, security auditing, and release engineering.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (runs on CPU as Markdown prompt definitions, Bash/TypeScript scripts, and Bun/Node CLI tools).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~512 MB – 2 GB system RAM**.\n\n---\n\n### 12. **handy**\n- **Summary:** Handy is a free, open-source cross-platform desktop application built with Tauri (Rust + React) that provides private, offline speech-to-text transcription. It allows users to press a global shortcut, transcribe speech locally using Whisper or CPU-optimized Parakeet V3 models with Silero VAD, and directly paste text into active applications.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (runs efficiently on CPU using Parakeet V3 or CPU Whisper via GGML/GGUF, with optional GPU acceleration supported).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~1 GB – 4 GB system RAM** (depending on the selected Whisper or Parakeet model size).\n\n---\n\n### 13. **agent-browser**\n- **Summary:** agent-browser is a fast native Rust CLI and daemon designed for AI agents to automate and interact with web browsers via accessibility tree snapshots and refs. It enables AI assistants to open pages, click/fill elements, take annotated screenshots, execute JavaScript, and stream browser sessions with minimal token overhead.\n- **Requires GPU:** **No** (runs on CPU using headless Chrome/Chromium automation).\n- **Minimum RAM:** **~1 GB – 2 GB system RAM** for headless Chrome and daemon operation.","Recipient":"b75d3ef6-c7ea-497e-a92f-fe05edd5e5c3","toolAction":"Sending research results to caller","toolSummary":"Send READM